  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 306 PM EDT Thu Aug 13 2026\n\nGOES Day Cloud Phase Distinction shows that this morning's cirrus \nhas nearly rotated out of the CWA as the Plains mesoscale system has \npropagated eastward, and a popcorn cumulus deck has emerged \nover the UP. Temperatures are hitting the low- to mid-80s across\nthe southern tier of the UP, while some shoreline-adjacent \nareas with onshore surface flow show temperatures in the high \n60s to low 70s. Dewpoints sit in the high 50s inland, and above \n60F in coastal spots. For the remainder of today, HREF 6-hr LPMM\nshows a few isolated locations receiving above 0.05\" between \nnoon and 6 pm (the window of highest totals for today), and the \nmost recent HRRR runs have evolved with diminishing \nreflectivities in the MQT CWA. Even with the lake breeze making \nits way past Ishpeming as of 1815Z, the rest of today should be \ndry for the vast majority of the CWA.\n\nOver the next few days, ensembles agree that 500 mb ridging builds \ninto the area as the Gulf ridge amplifies and extends anomalously \nhigh heights through the Great Lakes region and into the province of \nQuebec. At 850 mb on Friday evening, a trough emerges within the \nensemble solutions over the province of Alberta, which then is \nsteered southeast and reaches the Upper Great Lakes by Saturday \nafternoon. This is the instigating feature of Saturday's widespread \nprecipitation chances. LREF mean 24-hr precipitation from 12Z Sat to \n12Z Sun shows 0.15\"-0.20\" of accumulation for most of the UP, and up \nto 0.33\" for areas near Menominee County and the Garden Peninsula. \nThe 90th percentile for this same time period shows up to 0.9\" \ninches at the most, once again near Menominee County. From the NBM, \nboth the 50th and 90th percentiles of these distributions have \ndecreased notably since the same model runs from 24 hours previous. \nThis trend adds confidence to lower accumulation totals for this \nweekend's rain in the UP. As far as thunderstorm chances go, LREF \nMUCAPE shows 25-60% chance of CAPE>250 J/kg for Saturday evening, \nand 25-50% chance of the same for Sunday afternoon. This shows \nminimal change from the LREF of 24 hours previous, and concurs with \nSPC's General Thunderstorms Day 3 Outlook.\n\nAmong impacts from this weak surface low are overnight dewpoints on \nSaturday. LREF shows 55-80% chance of dewpoints exceeding 60F \novernight on Saturday (excepting points north of Portal Canal, whose \nexceedance probabilities are less than 40%). Ensemble spread \nduring this period ranges from 5-8F, translating to high \nprobabilities overnight dewpoints will be somewhere in the 60s. \n\nBy Sunday, this surface low flattens the robust ridging anchored in \nthe Gulf and 500 mb flow goes quasi-zonal from the NW. LREF \nclustering solutions for 500 mb heights indicate that this gradient \nflow remains until around Aug 18. At this point, LREF clusters begin \nto present diverging solutions, with some showing a surface trough \nand accompanying precipitation chances entering into the picture for \nAug 18-19, while others show ridging. Thus, uncertainty still \nremains for the latter half of the forecast period.",
